{
  "id": "grammar.a2.20.01.perfecto",
  "title": "Pretérito Perfecto Review",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content": "Use the pretérito perfecto for recent experiences and actions connected to the present. Form it with haber + past participle: he aprendido, hemos conocido, han compartido. It is useful when the experience matters now or when the time period is still connected to the present."
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "spanish": "Hemos aprendido mucho.",
          "english": "We have learnt a lot."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "He conocido a personas increíbles.",
          "english": "I have met incredible people."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Hemos compartido muchos recuerdos.",
          "english": "We have shared many memories."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "¿Has terminado el curso?",
          "english": "Have you finished the course?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        [
          "he aprendido",
          "I have learnt"
        ],
        [
          "hemos conocido",
          "we have met"
        ],
        [
          "han compartido",
          "they have shared"
        ],
        [
          "has terminado",
          "you have finished"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "tip",
      "content": "Ask what time frame you are describing. Use the pretérito perfecto for recent experiences, the indefinido for completed past events, ir a + infinitive for plans, and the futuro simple for predictions and future goals."
    },
    {
